---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Field Guide"
  description="What I've found inside the game so far — file formats, art types, dialog system, naming canon. Plain English, no romhacker jargon (or if I have to use it, I'll explain)."
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Field Guide' }]}
>
  <p>
    This is the part of the archive where I write down what I've pulled apart
    and how it works. The game is a Japan-only DS title with no English
    release, so most of what's here was learned by opening files in a hex
    editor, decompiling code, and writing tools to parse formats. Each section
    below covers one slice of that.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pages link to each other where it makes sense, so it's fine to wander in
    from any direction. If a page assumes a term you don't know, it'll either
    define it inline or link to the page that does.
  </p>

  <div class="fg-cards">
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>2D Art</h3>
      <p>The kinds of images in the game — item icons, magic glyphs, NPC portraits, background screens, the 63-page magazine system, and the 140 NPC stamps — and the rules for finding the right palette for each.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>File Formats</h3>
      <p>Every container and wire format I've decoded: RESO, RESC, LZ11, the Nintendo Nitro graphics formats, the game's custom Konami extensions, and the SZL-wrapped <code>recipe/</code> folder.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>How the Game Works</h3>
      <p>How the engine actually runs: the overlay system (with the per-overlay knowledgebase), the content cache, how a 2D UI screen gets composed from a recipe, and how sprites reach the screen.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>The Message System</h3>
      <p>How dialog is stored. The message wire format with its opcodes and color tags, plus the shared-payload trick the game uses to save space on repeated lines.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Names &amp; Words</h3>
      <p>The 377 magic glyphs on the player's keyboard (sourced from <code>2d/inputmagic/msg.ofs</code>), the cooking recipe slots with their variant keys, and the ARM9 menu pool that holds short action verbs like "Hold" and "Place."</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Translation Canon</h3>
      <p>What we keep consistent across the patch. NPC names, currency, royal titles, class names, and how Game 1's official English localization anchors our choices.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    The Field Guide is the "how it works" layer. Several of the topics it
    introduces have their own dedicated sections in the archive with the
    actual data, tooling, or browsable catalog behind the explanation:
  </p>
  <ul class="fg-pointers">
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlays/`}><strong>Per-overlay catalog</strong></a> —
      for deeper technical detail on each of the 180 overlays. Per-overlay
      pages with function listings, container references, and embedded JP
      strings, indexed and searchable. Lives inside "How the Game Works"
      under the Field Guide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}/save-files/`}><strong>Save-file editor</strong></a> —
      for inspecting and editing your own save. Drop in a .sav / .dsv;
      what we currently know about the save format is rendered there,
      with edit affordances for the fields we've verified.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}/translation/`}><strong>Translation browser</strong></a> —
      for searching every dialog line in the game. JP source, working EN,
      per-row status, filterable by file and speaker.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}><strong>Changelog</strong></a> — for the
      full version history with before/after diffs of every row that
      changed in each release.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${base}/patches/`}><strong>Patches</strong></a> — for the
      latest patch download and the MD5 / Delta Patcher how-to.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A note on confidence</h2>
  <p>
    Some of what's here is verified at the byte level — round-trip tested,
    pixel-exact, or confirmed by decompiling the engine code that reads it.
    Other parts are working theories that fit the data but haven't been
    proven yet. Where it matters, I try to flag confidence inline. If a page
    says "verified," I've seen the code that does the thing. If it says
    "working theory," it explains the data we have but might still be wrong.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>

<style>
  .fg-pointers {
    margin: 12px 0 24px;
    padding-left: 22px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .fg-pointers li {
    margin: 8px 0;
  }
  .fg-pointers a strong {
    color: var(--color-purple-600);
  }
</style>
